<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limoges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustoritum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Limoges is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> river in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the historic capital of the Limousin. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Augustoritum, it took its name from the Gallic Lemovices. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was famed for its enamel work and as a stage on the pilgrimage road to Compostela, and from the 18th century it became the leading centre of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limoges.jpg" alt="Limoges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Babsy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre-ville_et_champ_de_Juillet.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of settlement before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. They founded the city around 10 BCE as Augustoritum, the second part of the name being Gaulish for ford, as part of Augustus's reorganisation of the province. The Roman city had an amphitheatre measuring 136 by 115 metres, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a forum, baths and several sanctuaries, and it had a Senate and a currency of its own. Saint Martial is said to have evangelised it around 250. In the late 3rd century, as Germanic invasions made life unsafe, the population withdrew to a site that was easier to fortify, the modern Puy Saint-Étienne.</p><p>From the 11th century the Abbey of St. Martial and its large library made Limoges an artistic centre, home to a school of medieval music composition. In the 13th century the town, ruled by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and the castle, with its abbey, stood as two fortified settlements, sometimes at odds. In 1370 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward of Woodstock</a>, the Black Prince, occupied the town and massacred some 300 residents. The two settlements were united in 1792, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the population destroyed the abbey of St. Martial among other religious buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Limoges is known for its crafts. Enamels from the city were exported throughout the Christian world from the 12th century, and the discovery of kaolin near Saint-Yrieix-la-Perche in the 18th century began its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> industry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">Leather</a> work also settled along the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, and luxury shoes, gloves and bags are still made in Limoges and Saint-Junien. The city has a tradition of voting on the left and of workers' movements; the Confédération Générale du Travail, the first French confederation of workers, was created there in 1895, and strikes in 1905 led to barricades and the death of a young porcelain worker, Camille Vardelle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Gothic cathedral of Saint-Étienne was begun in 1273 and finished only in 1888. The crypt of Saint Martial, of the 10th century, was found in the 1960s during building work, and remains of the Gallo-Roman amphitheatre, one of the largest in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, survive. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> were evacuated to the area, and Limoges was liberated on 21 August 1944 by the Resistance led by Colonel Georges Guingouin, without fighting. The city has held the label City of Art and History since 2008 and joined the UNESCO Creative Cities Network in 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Limoges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes commanded the service of their vassals, the counts and viscounts of Angoulême, Périgord, La Marche and <b>Limoges</b> and the lords of the castles that multiplied across the region from the late tenth century, but these vassals were often in revolt, and much ducal warfare was spent…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome their capital was <b>Augustoritum</b>, later Lemovices, from which come Limoges and the Limousin.</blockquote><a href="/state/lemovices">Lemovices</a></li><li><blockquote>Viscounts appear at <b>Limoges</b> in the late 9th century as deputies of the counts of Poitou, who as dukes of Aquitaine claimed overlordship of the Limousin.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Viscounty of Limoges</a></li></ul></section>
